=", "text": "Goibniu is an Irish god. He is the smith of the Tuatha Dé Danann and one of three gods of craft. He forged a new silver arm for King Nuada after battle struck off his old one. The arm worked like a real limb. Some versions credit Dian Cécht. A blemished king had to resign, so Bres took the throne. Goibniu killed Ruadán, son of Bres and Brigit, who came to spy. Ruadán's mother invented keening, the wild sound of grief. Goibniu healed his own wounds at the sacred well on Slane hill. He owned the Glas Gaibhleann, the cow of abundance, and lived in County Cavan. Folklore carries him on as Goban Saor, a clever smith. His cauldron's guests drank and grew ever younger.", "okr": {"objective": "Describe Goibniu as the smith-god of the Tuatha Dé Danann and a master of craft.", "key_results": ["Goibniu forged Nuada a silver arm that worked like a real one.", "He killed Ruadán, which led Ruadán's mother to invent keening.", "He healed himself at the sacred well on Slane hill.", "He owned the cow of abundance and lived on as Goban Saor."], "evidence": "Monaghan 2008, s.v. 'Goibniu'."}}
